    commentaryText: `ST. AUGUSTINE OF HIPPO: Consider the tender comparison chosen by Divine Wisdom! Among all birds, none displays more visible maternal solicitude than the hen: she droops her wings, roughens her feathers, becomes hoarse in her voice, and weakens herself out of love for her chicks to shield them from the hawk. So too the Eternal Son of God assumed our weak flesh, drooped the wings of His majesty upon the Cross, and spread out His pierced arms to shelter us from the infernal predator.

ST. CYRIL OF ALEXANDRIA: Yet He says, "How often I yearned to gather your children, and you were unwilling!" Grace calls with infinite tenderness, yet never destroys human free will. Let us hasten beneath the wings of Christ today as we sing at the Sanctus of the Mass: "Blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord!"

CORNELIUS À LAPIDE, S.J.: "How often would I have gathered thy children together, as the hen doth gather her brood under her wings." Lapide explains that the hen is the only bird that weakens herself, droops her wings, and cries out in distress when her chicks are threatened by the hawk. So Christ weakened Himself in the Incarnation and stretched out His arms on the Cross to shield mankind from the infernal dragon.

CATECHISM OF THE CATHOLIC CHURCH (CCC 558): Jesus' lamentation over Jerusalem expresses the sorrow of God's rejected love. Yet His desire to gather all under His wings is fulfilled when He surrenders Himself voluntarily on the Cross for the salvation of the world.

HOMILY OF ST. BERNARD OF CLAIRVAUX (ON THE PROTECTIVE WINGS OF CHRIST): How often would I have gathered thy children together as a hen gathers her brood under her wings! O wondrous condescension of the Divine King! Like a mother hen, Christ exposed His own body to the sharp talons of the infernal hawk on the Cross to shield us beneath His wounded side. Hide yourself beneath those sacred wings in every temptation, and no enemy shall snatch you from His hand.`,
    practicalApplication: 'Take refuge today beneath the outstretched arms of Jesus on the Cross and in the Tabernacle, surrendering your stubborn will to His protective love.'
