    commentaryText: `POPE ST. GREGORY THE GREAT: Dropsy is a disease in which the more a man drinks, the more parched with thirst he becomes. Fittingly, therefore, the man with dropsy healed in the Pharisee's house represents the avaricious soul: the more worldly riches or honors he swallows, the more feverishly he thirsts for more. Only the touch of Christ can cool the fever of covetousness and restore the soul to spiritual health.

THE VENERABLE BEDE: By asking whether it is lawful to heal on the Sabbath, the Lord exposes the cold hypocrisy of those who prioritized animal property over human salvation. True Sabbath rest is perfected in active charity.

CORNELIUS À LAPIDE, S.J.: "A man before Him who had the dropsy." Lapide notes that the dropsical man suffers from an insatiable thirst caused by internal fluid accumulation. Spiritually, this represents the miser or the ambitious man: the more wealth or honor he accumulates, the more insatiably he thirsts for more. Christ heals him on the Sabbath to prove that works of divine mercy are the true fulfillment of holy rest.

CATECHISM OF THE CATHOLIC CHURCH (CCC 2173): On the Sabbath, Jesus shows that the law of love and corporal works of mercy supersede ritualism. By healing on the Sabbath, He restores the true meaning of the Lord's Day as a day of liberation and charity.

HOMILY OF ST. CYRIL OF ALEXANDRIA (ON THE MAN WITH DROPSY): The Lord heals on the Sabbath day to teach us that divine mercy knows no repose, for God's nature is eternal goodness. The man afflicted with dropsy represents those whose hearts are swollen with worldly pride and insatiable desire. By His touch, Christ drains away the poisonous moisture of greed and restores spiritual health to the soul.`,
