    commentaryText: `ST. CYRIL OF ALEXANDRIA: Notice the insane folly of this man who said within himself: "What shall I do, for I have nowhere to store my crops?" The earth was fruitful, but his soul was barren. He had vast granaries of grain, but not a single room in his heart for the poor! He considered only his belly, his barns, his pleasures: "Soul, you have many good things laid up for many years; rest, eat, drink, be merry." O foolish man! Do you have a soul of swine that you feed it with earthly harvests? The food of the soul is faith, virtue, prayer, and charity!

ST. BASIL THE GREAT: "I will pull down my barns," he says, "and build larger ones." Why destroy the barns you already have? Because greed knows no measure. You have hungry neighbors at your doorstep, shivering orphans, widows weeping in distress—these are the real barns of the rich! The bread you hold back belongs to the hungry; the coat keeping in your closet belongs to the naked; the shoes rotting in your house belong to the barefoot.

ST. AUGUSTINE OF HIPPO: God says to him: "This night your life will be demanded of you." He was planning for many years, but had not even one night remaining! He who is rich toward God stores his treasures in the hands of Christ through almsgiving, which are carried directly to the eternal kingdom.

CORNELIUS À LAPIDE, S.J.: "Thou fool, this night thy soul shall be required of thee." Lapide writes that the rich man was called a fool because he mistook temporal goods for his final end, calculated on many future years without owning a single second, and forgot that wealth belongs to God and is entrusted to man for almsgiving to the poor.

VATICAN II (GAUDIUM ET SPES 69): God intended the earth with everything contained in it for the use of all human beings and peoples. Therefore, created goods should be in abundance for all in an equitable manner. Those in abundance are obliged to relieve the poor.

HOMILY OF ST. JOHN CHRYSOSTOM (ON THE RICH FOOL): O rich man, why do you tear down your barns to build larger ones, when the stomachs of the poor are waiting to receive your surplus? Your granaries will rot, your wealth will pass to strangers, and this very night your soul shall be demanded of you. Be rich toward God! Store up your gold in the hands of the poor, and you will find it waiting for you as incorruptible treasure in heaven.`,
