    commentaryText: `ST. JOHN CHRYSOSTOM: Consider the infinite love of our Redeemer! He gave us His own Flesh to eat, that He might draw us into the closest union with Himself. As food becomes one with the body, so Christ becomes one with the soul that receives Him in holy faith and charity.

ST. CYRIL OF ALEXANDRIA: Doubt not whether this be true, since He plainly says, "This is My body," but rather receive the word of the Savior with faith; for since He is the Truth, He does not lie. By Holy Communion He dwells in us and we in Him, bestowing immortal life upon our mortal flesh.

CORNELIUS À LAPIDE, S.J.: Observe here against all Sacramentarians and heretics that Christ is speaking not metaphorically of spiritual eating by mere faith, but literally of sacramental eating of His real Flesh in the Holy Eucharist. First, because when the Jews murmured, Christ did not correct them as though they had taken a metaphor literally, but confirmed it with a solemn oath ("Amen, amen"). Second, He adds: "For My flesh is true food, and My blood is true drink" (alēthōs brōsis). The Greek word alēthōs means truly, really, substantially.

VATICAN II (SACROSANCTUM CONCILIUM 47 / CCC 1374): At the Last Supper Our Savior instituted the Eucharistic sacrifice of His Body and Blood. In the most blessed sacrament of the Eucharist, the Body and Blood, together with the soul and divinity, of our Lord Jesus Christ and, therefore, the whole Christ is truly, really, and substantially contained.

HOMILY OF ST. THOMAS AQUINAS (ON THE MOST HOLY EUCHARIST): O priceless banquet, offered for our salvation! The Only-Begotten Son of God, wishing to make us partakers of His divinity, took our human nature and offered His Body to God the Father on the Altar of the Cross. And that the memory of so great a benefit might abide with us forever, He left us His Flesh for food and His Blood for drink under the species of bread and wine. He who eats My flesh and drinks My blood abides in Me, and I in him.`,
